# Target B - exact three-phase cross-grain determinant floor

Date: 2026-10-02
Base main: 7c95482d3a0c1c0df7319aa70a0f5fe0878d7707
Status: proved candidate, noncanonical. Target B remains open.

This note strengthens the cross-grain virtual-mass bound in
research/target_b_cross_grain_virtual_determinant_2026-10-02.md.
It is independent of the current Target C-II high-t fan / boundary-rhombus work.

Fix Section-50 parameters 0 < eta, lambda < 1. Delete crossings with angular
defect greater than D^(-1+eta), then perform the Section-50 vertex pruning.
Let H_i and H_j be two distinct retained components. Write their three phase
class sizes, in decreasing order, as

    M_i >= b_i >= a_i
    M_j >= b_j >= a_j

and put

    N_i = M_i + b_i + a_i
    N_j = M_j + b_j + a_j.

As in the preceding cross-grain note, minimum retained degree gives

    b_i, b_j >= lambda D / 2.                              (1)

Let

    L0 = ceil(3T/(lambda D))
    epsilon_D = L0 D^(-1+eta) = o(1).                     (2)

The Section-50 potential and signed lift formula put every train in phase class r
of a retained component within projective angle epsilon_D of one ideal direction

    alpha + r pi/3.

## 1. Exact weighted three-phase trigonometric lemma

For nonnegative A, B, C, define

    f(theta)
      = A |sin theta|
      + B |sin(theta + pi/3)|
      + C |sin(theta - pi/3)|.

Then

    min_theta f(theta)
      = (sqrt(3)/2) [A + B + C - max(A,B,C)].             (3)

Proof. The zeros of the three absolute-sine terms occur at multiples of pi/3.
On every open interval between consecutive zeros, all three signs are fixed, so

    f'' = -f < 0.

Thus f is concave on every such interval and its minimum there is attained at an
endpoint. At the three endpoint types the values are

    (sqrt(3)/2)(A+B),
    (sqrt(3)/2)(B+C),
    (sqrt(3)/2)(C+A).

Their minimum is exactly the right side of (3).

The earlier four-direction inequality is the special case (A,B,C) = (2,1,1).

## 2. Exact ideal determinant mass of two grains

Keep the actual cyclic phase labels temporarily. Let

    x_r = |V_r(H_i)|
    y_r = |V_r(H_j)|

for r in Z/3, and define the three cyclic correlations

    C_d = sum_r x_r y_(r+d),  d in Z/3.                   (4)

If the ideal phase triples have relative angle theta, the determinant mass of all
nine phase-class products is exactly

    F_ij(theta)
      = C_0 |sin theta|
      + C_1 |sin(theta + pi/3)|
      + C_-1 |sin(theta - pi/3)|.                         (5)

Since

    C_0 + C_1 + C_-1 = N_i N_j,

equation (3) gives

    F_ij(theta)
      >= (sqrt(3)/2) [N_i N_j - max_d C_d].               (6)

Each C_d pairs the three class sizes of H_i with a permutation of the three class
sizes of H_j. By the rearrangement inequality,

    max_d C_d <= M_i M_j + b_i b_j + a_i a_j.             (7)

Therefore

    F_ij(theta)
      >= (sqrt(3)/2)
         [N_i N_j - M_i M_j - b_i b_j - a_i a_j].         (8)

The bracket equals

    M_i(a_j+b_j)
      + (a_i+b_i)M_j
      + a_i b_j
      + b_i a_j.                                          (9)

In particular,

    F_ij(theta)
      >= (sqrt(3)/2)(M_i b_j + b_i M_j).                  (10)

This recovers the previous sqrt(3) b_i b_j floor because M_i >= b_i and
M_j >= b_j.

## 3. Actual train directions

Define

    W_ij
      = sum |det(u_tau,u_sigma)|

over pairs tau in H_i and sigma in H_j that do not physically cross.

For each pair, the actual relative angle differs from its ideal phase-pair angle
by at most 2 epsilon_D. Since |sin| is 1-Lipschitz, replacing all actual
directions by their ideal phase directions loses at most

    2 epsilon_D N_i N_j.                                  (11)

If a train of H_i physically crosses a train of H_j, that crossing was removed
in the initial high-defect deletion. Both endpoints survive pruning; any retained
low-defect crossing between them would still be an edge and would put them in the
same retained component. Removing physical crossing pairs loses at most their
number because every determinant is at most one.

Let E_hi(H_i,H_j) be the initial high-defect crossings between the two retained
vertex sets. Then

    W_ij
      >= (sqrt(3)/2)
         [N_i N_j - M_i M_j - b_i b_j - a_i a_j]
         - 2 epsilon_D N_i N_j
         - |E_hi(H_i,H_j)|.                               (12)

Hence

    W_ij
      >= (sqrt(3)/2)(M_i b_j + b_i M_j) - o(D^2).         (13)

Using (1),

    W_ij
      >= (sqrt(3) lambda D / 4)(M_i + M_j) - o(D^2).      (14)

Thus a phase-heavy macroscopic grain is more expensive, not less expensive, to
keep separate from another retained grain. The previous b_i b_j estimate
discarded this information.

## 4. Global form

Let

    V_cross = sum_(i<j) W_ij.

For fixed lambda, Section 50 gives O_lambda(1) retained components,
N_i = O(D), and epsilon_D = o(1). The high-defect crossing sets are disjoint
across component pairs and have total size O(D^(2-eta)) = o(D^2). Summing (12),

    V_cross
      >= (sqrt(3)/2) sum_(i<j)
         [N_i N_j - M_i M_j - b_i b_j - a_i a_j]
         - o(D^2).                                        (15)

Equivalently,

    V_cross
      >= (sqrt(3)/2) sum_(i<j)
         [M_i(a_j+b_j)
          + (a_i+b_i)M_j
          + a_i b_j
          + b_i a_j]
         - o(D^2).                                        (16)

If H_* is any retained component, its pairs with all other retained components
alone contribute

    V_cross
      >= (sqrt(3)/2) M_* sum_(j != *) b_j - o(D^2)
      >= (sqrt(3) lambda D / 4)(k-1) M_* - o(D^2).        (17)

## 5. Zonotope budget consequence

The exact train-zonotope identity gives

    V_cross <= V_miss
            = area(Z) - area(R).

For a pure disk, per(Z) = 2T, so area(Z) <= T^2/pi. Section 36 and the strict
survivor shell window give

    area(R) = pi D^2 / 4 - O(D).

Therefore

    (sqrt(3)/2) sum_(i<j)
      [N_i N_j - M_i M_j - b_i b_j - a_i a_j]
      <= T^2/pi - pi D^2/4 + o(D^2).                      (18)

This strictly strengthens the previous bound

    sqrt(3) sum_(i<j) b_i b_j
      <= T^2/pi - pi D^2/4 + o(D^2),

because every bracket in (18) is at least 2 b_i b_j.

## 6. Scope and remaining bottleneck

This is a proof-only strengthening of the noncanonical Target B multi-grain
route. It does not alter theorem-state authority and does not close Target B.

The cross-grain virtual determinant mass now sees the full three-phase population
vector rather than only the two second-largest classes. In particular a retained
component cannot evade the virtual-area budget merely by placing most of its trains
in one phase class.

The remaining obstruction is still the physical conversion. The virtual mass in
(15)-(18) is not automatically Oler shell surplus. A closing theorem must couple
this stronger cross-grain mass to Section-50 deleted/interface crossings and retained
physical crossings, or turn it into a strict smaller-diameter competitor.
